      "Nadal's final competitive appearance came in the opening singles rubber of the tie, where he battled fiercely before falling 6-4, 6-4 to Dutch number one Botic van de Zandschulp. Despite the outcome, the 11,500 fans in attendance chanted his name relentlessly throughout the match, honoring a champion whose career redefined the boundaries of athletic resilience. Following the subsequent defeat of Spain's doubles tandem, a grand post-match ceremony was held on the court, featuring a commemorative video tribute with personal messages from his greatest rivals and contemporaries: Roger Federer, Novak Djokovic, Serena Williams, and soccer icon Andres Iniesta.",
      "Over a glittering twenty-three-year career on the ATP Tour, the 'King of Clay' captured 22 Grand Slam singles titles—including an unfathomable 14 championships on the red dirt of Roland Garros in Paris, a record widely regarded by historians as the single most dominant athletic feat in the annals of individual sports. His career achievements also encompass 92 ATP singles titles, an Olympic singles gold medal in Beijing (2008), an Olympic doubles gold in Rio (2016), five Davis Cup crowns for Spain, and 209 weeks ranked as the world's number one player.",
      "Addressing the weeping crowd through choked tears, Nadal was characteristically humble, emphasizing that he wanted to be remembered above all as a good person who gave everything to his craft. 'The titles, the numbers are there, and people probably know that,' Nadal said during his farewell speech. 'But the way I would like to be remembered more is as a good person from a small village in Mallorca who followed his dreams and worked as hard as possible to achieve what I achieved. I leave with the peace of mind that I gave everything, that I made an effort in every single match until the very end.'",
      "Federer, who shared an iconic rivalry and deep personal friendship with Nadal that captivated the sports world for nearly two decades, penned an eloquent open letter honoring his former foe: 'You made the whole tennis world proud. What an incredible run you've had. Including 14 French Opens—historic! You made Spain proud... you made the whole tennis world proud.' Novak Djokovic and Andy Murray similarly praised Nadal's ferocious fighting spirit, which set the gold standard for athletic professionalism across an entire generation of athletes.",
      "As Nadal walked off the court for the final time, bag slung over his shoulder with his hand raised to the rafters, the global sports community stood united in appreciation. His retirement marks the fading sunset of the golden era of men's tennis, but the legacy of his humility, respect for opponents, and legendary ferocity on court will inspire generations of athletes for decades to come."
