      "In the five-page directive, Scharf instructed department leadership that no agency personnel may submit testimony, respond to formal committee inquiries, or negotiate appearance schedules with congressional staff independently. Instead, all congressional outreach must be routed through a newly instituted Executive Oversight Review Board housed within the West Wing. The memorandum argues that uncoordinated congressional compliance risks compromising executive privilege, executive branch constitutional prerogatives, and ongoing administrative policy implementations.",
      "'The executive branch will not be intimidated, extorted, or disrupted by politically motivated and legally unauthorized fishing expeditions,' the memorandum asserts. 'While the administration recognizes the legitimate legislative inquiries of Congress, oversight must serve an authentic legislative purpose under Article I. Congressional inquiries that cross into harassment, personal intimidation, or interference with the core constitutional duties of the President and his appointees will be vigorously resisted and met with total institutional discipline.'",
      "The sweeping policy directive arrives amidst a bitter war of words between the White House and Rep. Robert Garcia of California, the incoming Democratic chairman of the House Committee on Oversight and Accountability. Days prior, Scharf sent a scathing warning letter to Garcia, asserting that aggressive threats of subpoenas against administration officials could 'cross the line into blatant criminality' if designed to impede federal duties. Garcia and incoming Democratic leaders swiftly condemned the memorandum on Tuesday as an unlawful effort to evade constitutional accountability and shield executive misconduct from the American public.",
      "'The Constitution does not grant the President or his personal lawyers the right to place an administrative gag order on public servants who are accountable to the taxpayers,' Garcia said in a statement from Capitol Hill. 'Article I of the United States Constitution vests Congress with the sole power of legislative oversight. The administration cannot unilaterally declare itself exempt from federal subpoenas, financial transparency, and legal accountability. When the 120th Congress convenes, our committees will execute their constitutional mandate with absolute resolve.'"
